<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Messiah (Handel)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1741</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Messiah is an English-language oratorio by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-frideric-handel">George Frideric Handel</a>, with a text compiled from the Bible by Charles Jennens. Composed in 1741, it received its first performance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> on 13 April 1742, at a charity concert in the Music Hall on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fishamble-street">Fishamble Street</a>. It became one of the most frequently performed choral works in the Western tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/messiah-handel.jpg" alt="Messiah (Handel)" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Frideric Handel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Messiah-titlepage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
